now ed miliband leads the charge on  getting to net zero for the labour party  but it does feel like climate change may  be slipping down the priority list as  the cost of living crisis really starts  to bite so are people in pain from  storing energy costs is going green  still a vote winner for the party and  what would it do to help those  struggling to get by well let's talk now  to labour's shadow climate change  secretary ed miliband thank you very  much for being uh with us uh you want to  put down this amendment to the queen's  speech on a windfall tax just explain  the thinking behind it  frankly we face a social emergency in  this country sophie  seven million people we know are  skipping meals because they can't afford  their bills energy bills are rocketing  upwards  and at the same time as a direct result  of that we've got oil and gas companies  making billions of pounds literally  record profits  of course the right thing to do is to  levy a windfall tax on those oil and gas  companies so we can provide proper help  to families 200 pounds across the board  cutting bills for all families 600  pounds for 9 million families who are  facing the biggest struggles and that is  absolutely essential i will say to you i  think it is frankly obscene that the  government is refusing to do this i mean  i i listened to your quasi-quieting  interview i'm not interested in their  internal machinations about this because  every day that goes by when they refuse  to do the right thing is another day  when millions of people in this country  have sleepless nights about how they're  going to afford their bills my message  to the chancellor is this you're going  to do a windfall tax i believe he is  going to do a windfall tax because  frankly it's an unanswerable case get on  with it and do it and bring real help to  families  um at the same time i just wonder um  obviously you're someone who is pushing  the climate changer agenda something  that many of our viewers will be very  concerned about as well but at a time  when bills are going up a lot of these  measures heat pumps for example costing  10 000 pounds a lot of people will be  thinking look  i love the idea of going green but right  now i simply can't afford it and what  would you say to those people  well here's the thing sophie because  this is what's changed you see the  answer to the climate crisis we face is  also the answer to the cost of living  crisis we face labor's got a plan to  insulate 19 million homes over the next  10 years 2 million in the coming year  that would cut bills cut carbon  emissions  and create jobs if you take the green  energy sprint which we need and the  government is not doing onshore wind  solar are now our cheapest forms of  power we should be driving ahead with  those things we should be getting rid of  ridiculous planning rules in england  which stop onshore win that would cut  carbon emissions and cut bills for  consumers so the old debate was shall we  go green it'll be more expensive  actually the new reality is going green  is now the cheaper option and i'm afraid  the government's half-hearted approach  to these climate issues is not just bad  for the climate and it is terrible for  the climber it's also bad when it comes  to affordability and the cost of living  crisis as well as energy security  because we can cut our gas imports too  if actually we go on this green spring  at the same time though labour's green  new deal that would cost 28 billion  pounds every year i mean that's one  percent of uk gdp and to put that in  context you know the famous windfall tax  that you talk about in every a labor  politician on the show talks about that  would raise about 2 billion pounds at  best so you would need 14 windfall taxes  to pay for one year of the green new  deal i mean is this really  realistic no i'm very oh let me be very  let me very very clear about this we  think sophie that it is right to borrow  to invest that 28 billion pounds and let  me explain why  because the biggest challenge and the  big the most improved thing we could do  is fail to invest in tackling the  climate crisis because we will store up  massive costs for the future don't take  my word for it the office of budget  responsibility produce an excellent  report and you know i'm a bit nerdy so  i've kind of looked at their report in  detail that shows if we delay by a  decade if we delay climate action by a  decade we double the cost of going green  let me give you a very concrete example  on this just just to show you what it  means  because the government got rid of a zero  carbon home standard which would have  meant that all new homes would have been  built zero carbon we've built a million  homes since then we're going to have to  insulate retrofit those homes that's  going to cost us more money so it's  another example of why the prudent  choice the sensible economic choice the  sensible fiscal choice as well as the  ethical choice is to invest in in  tackling the climate emergency and  there's a huge potential of jobs in this  country one of which just made this  point one of one of the problems i have  with government is that they have no  plan for growth if we invest that money  in the automotive sector in electric  cars so we can get ahead in that in  steel so it can be transformed in new  industries like hydrogen it can create  good jobs at good wages the biggest  challenge we face the biggest long-term  economic challenge we face  if you're being honest with people  though wouldn't you spend a little bit  less time talking about the windfall tax  that raises two billion a year and spend  a bit more time being upfront about how  the fact like as you have just been that  labor would effectively be borrowing  billions and billions more pounds every  year to pay for this 28 billion  a year green new deal i'm being i'm  being i'm being extremely open and i'm  very happy to talk about both but let me  make this point to you  the reason why this matters is we have a  short-term plan and a long-term plan and  the short-term plan is really important  i do want to underline this point sophia  i was in assistance advice  office in my constituency on friday  meeting a couple and a single person who  are facing this cost of living christ  and let me just underline this this  point to you  these were people this was one set of  people who are homeowners and who are  deeply worried about how they'd afford  to pay their bills and another lady who  was renting our house on universal  credit incredibly worried  frankly just no hope of paying her  direct debit  this you know i know that we often talk  about emergencies and difficulties and  so on in my 30 years or so that i've  been involved in politics i have never  known a cost of living crunch like this  we're set for the biggest fallen living  standards for 45 years in our country so  i i make no apologies for talking and  demanding demanding the government act  on the windfall tax because it is quite  wrong that they are not doing so  how concerned are you about the impact  the war in ukraine could have on energy  bills in the future  i i am concerned about it and and you  know  what this illustrates is that  if we are subject to fossil fuels if we  are reliant on fossil fuels then we are  basically at the mercy of global events  and there's a real lesson here because  this is a fossil fuel crisis  and the best way for us to have energy  security and energy security is  incredibly important is frankly to get  off fossil fuels because the thing about  onshore wind offshore wind nuclear power  solar energy all of those things is they  are home-grown sources of power which  aren't reliant on what is happening  globally what is happening around the  world and so that's why it's so  important that we that we um  that we go on this green energy sprint  okay um now just talking about ukraine  um you know the atrocities that vladimir  putin's committed in ukraine were in  many ways foreshadowed uh by what russia  did in syria you know it's part of the  same playbook mario poll seemingly like  another aleppo the general known as the  butcher of syria now leading the war  effort in ukraine  do you regret voting against military  action in syria back in 2013  uh no no i don't uh sophie look what i  was presented with when it came to what  was happening uh in syria  was a proposition  where we would not have been getting rid  of all the chemical weapons but this was  about the chemical weapons attack  that president assad had made we would  not be getting rid of all his chemical  weapons and there was no proper plan for  how we would be using british troops and  where this where our intervention would  go and we made a vow after iraq which  was that if we committed british troops  we would do so clear about our  objectives with a clear sense of a plan  and a clear sense that that plan would  be successful and our argument to david  cameron was  set out a clear plan how this is going  to work and what is going to mean and  those who say well it would have changed  the course of the war president trump  did actually bomb president assad in  relation to a chemical weapons attack a  few years later and it did not change  the course of the war so so of course  what has happened in syria is an  appalling tragedy but the idea that us  getting involved in another war in the  middle east would have stopped it  happening i just don't buy i'm afraid i  understand about your concerns about not  having a proper strategy um at the time  but was it also about sending a message  to people around the world that if you  commit war crimes and the west will  act and by not doing anything then you  also sent a message to people like putin  that the west wouldn't necessarily act  you know i remember in the election  campaign when you were a leader that  sort of famous moment when you spoke to  jeremy paxman you said hell yes i'm  tough enough talking about refusing to  back the airstrikes in syria that barack  obama wanted you said standing up to the  leader of the free world shows a certain  toughness i would say  do you think the real toughness comes  from standing up to leaders of the  unfree world  i just want to say you said at the  beginning of that question should we not  have engaged in military action in order  to send a message  i think we should learn some lessons  from our past on this  because  when terrible things happen around the  world yes we have a responsibility uh to  act in concert with our allies and we  are acting in relation to ukraine and  with the toughest economic sanctions  providing defensive equipment rightly to  the ukrainian regime against the  appalling acts of putin  but  when we commit british troops we don't  just do so to send a message we are  putting the young men and women of our  country in harm's way  and i think that leaders have a deep  responsibility to know that they are  doing so on the basis that we have a  chance of it succeeding we have a clear  plan  and we know where where this where the  intervention is going to go so and you  know in relation to libya i supported  david cameron in relation to  intervention uh in libya because i  thought there was a plan in relation to  the kind of humanitarian emergency that  there was there but i took my  responsibility  incredibly seriously as somebody who  wanted to be the prime minister of the  country that if i was going to commit  british troops it was on the right basis  in the right way  and with a clear chance of success  okay um now moving on to a few other  issues in the news recently sadiq khan  the mayor of london has set up a  commission to look into the potential  legalization of cannabis  would you like to see cannabis legalized  that isn't labour's position no  uh what what what we've said and look we  welcome sadiq looking at these issues  because this debate uh carry carries on  and should carry on he's looking at a  whole range of issues under i think it's  lord faulkner a distinguished person  very wise person on these issues uh to  look at issues of treatment and  education  and the wider issues that you mentioned  what's your name labor isn't  convinced well look you know i've said  in the past that there's something this  is something that needs to be looked at  you i think you probably uh uh know that  but labour's shadow cabinet position  which is a position i support as a  member of the shadow cabinet is to say  that we're not yet convinced of the case  uh for moving on decriminalization but  obviously we'll look at what lord  faulkner's commission has to say  okay and now we had the local elections  two weeks ago and i think it is fair to  say of course that kirsten made progress  compared to the jeremy corbyn  position  would you say he's now stuck in uh ed  miliband territory  i'm not i don't i don't think you mean  that as a compliment sophie so maybe i  should say no um look i think what um i  think what keir is doing is that we had  a terrible defeat in 2019  he has done a remarkable job i think in  bringing the party back from the brink  from what happened in 2019 putting us  back on the pitch let me tell you what i  detected as i went around the country in  the local elections  i think there is a big mood for change  in this country not just the cost of  living crisis this goes back much  further to brexit to the financial  crisis people feel the country's not  working for them now i think people are  increasingly convinced that the tories  aren't going to deliver and i have to  say listening to that quasi-quieting  interview it's no wonder people think  the tories can't deliver our job is to  show that we can bring that change and  that is a work in progress of course it  is we've got really important ideas like  the windfall tax like the climate  investment pledge and what you're going  to see and what i know kira is  determined to do is to show that burning  passion and that set of proposals to  change our country to bring the change  that i think people are crying out for  in this country you know remember after  the pandemic or during the pandemic  sophie we said we would build back  better all of us across politics  actually at the moment we're set to  build back far worse under this  government so we've got to show that we  can bring the change we need and i'm  convinced we can  okay thank you for taking that with uh  customary good humor it's appreciated uh  just finally before i let you go and  it's fair to say i think that his time  was going through one of the more  difficult  periods of his leadership  have you offered him any advice on it  i think all periods for um at the leader  of the opposition are challenging at  least in my experience sophie as you  know you were with me in the campaign of  2015. he doesn't need my advice you know  i think the thing about gear is that the  more people get to know him the more  they like what they see he's somebody of  great decency great integrity somebody  who cares deeply about this country what  what what drives kiev stammer in my view  what drives him is this country doesn't  work for millions of people across our  land this economy doesn't work for  millions of people across our country  and actually what his burning passion is  to change that economy to deliver good  jobs at decent wages to restore our  public services and to tackle the  climate crisis and create those good  jobs i was talking about and you know  the whole of the labour party is united  behind him in that mission  okay thank you uh very much uh indeed ed  miliband there thank you thank you

